  "content_text": "Fisheries and Aquaculture Minister Phil Heatley, has welcomed the latest litigation report from the Ministry which shows an increase in the number of people and companies prosecuted for fisheries related offences. Mr Heatley says effective enforcement is key to ensuring the sustainability of our fisheries. He has applauded the efforts of the more than 150 warranted officers and more than 200 honorary fisheries officers whose work led to nearly 400 criminal prosecutions being undertaken between August 2009 and August 2010. \"Of this figure around 280 offences related to recreational fishers, 74 related to poaching/black market offences, 38 to inshore commercial offences and a further 3 to deepwater commercial offences,\" says Mr Heatley. In the year to August, 11 offenders were sentenced to imprisonment, with sentences ranging from seven to 36 months. Seven offenders were sentenced to community detention or supervision ranging from two to nine months, and 16 offenders were sentenced to home detention for between two and 12 months. Two hundred and fifty-two prosecutions resulted in a fine and 94 in sentences of community work. One hundred and twenty one prosecutions involved forfeiture of boats, vehicles or equipment with a combined value of well over $10 million.* One vessel, the Kapitan Rusak, was valued at $9 million. The Ministry also issued 1,968 infringement notices for less serious offences, with 1,564 being served on recreational fishers and 404 on commercial fishers.  The fines for these infringements ranged from $250 to $750. \"Our fisheries are one of our nation's most valuable assets and it is important that everyone uses them responsibly. I am pleased to say the vast majority of fishers value the resource and do comply with the rules. However the Ministry of Fisheries will soon be employing a record number of honorary fisheries officers to deal with those who don't, with plans to engage a total of 225 officers by June 2011. \"This has been a huge overall effort by many people, I congratulate the Ministry and strongly encourage members of the public to work with them because we are all guardians of our fisheries,\" said Mr Heatley. Suspicious and illegal activity should be reported to the Ministry of Fisheries on 0800 4 POACHER - 0800 476 224. *This figure does not include sums recovered in relation to the criminal prosecutions for misreporting by companies and crew associated with the vessel Tomi Maru 87. Sentencing has yet to take place but $636,000 in sale proceeds from the illegal fishing has been forfeit to the Crown and a further $1.11m has been paid to the Crown by the owner of the Tomi Maru 87 in order to redeem it from seizure. Fisheries prosecutions by region 1 August 2009 - 31 July 2010 Region Court Number of prosecutions Region - totals Northland 28   Dargaville District Court 8     Kaikohe District Court 3     Kaitaia District Court 5     Whangarei District Court 12   Auckland 131   Auckland District Court 14     Manukau District Court 75     North Shore District Court 6     Papakura District Court 8     Pukekohe District Court 18     Waitakere District Court 6     Warkworth District Court 4   Waikato 18   Hamilton District Court 3     Thames District Court 11     Tokoroa District Court 3     Waihi District Court 1   Bay of Plenty 16   Opotiki District Court 3     Rotorua District Court 1     Tauranga District Court 12   Gisborne 19   Gisborne District Court 19   Hawke's Bay 14   Hastings District Court 9     Napier District Court 3     Wairoa District Court 2   Taranaki 18   Hawera District Court 11     New Plymouth District Court 7   Manawatu-Whanganui 5   Levin District Court 1     Whanganui District Court 4   Wellington 77   Lower Hutt District Court 14     Masterton District Court 6     Porirua District Court 25     Upper Hutt District Court 2     Wellington District Court 29     Wellington High Court 1   Tasman 0 Nelson 9   Nelson District Court 9   Marlborough 2   Blenheim District Court 2   West Coast 3   Greymouth District Court 2     Westport District Court 1   Canterbury 34   Christchurch District Court 20     Kaikoura District Court 9     Rangiora District Court 4     Timaru 1   Otago 4   Dunedin District Court 3     Oamaru District Court 1   Southland 17   Invercargill District Court 17",
